# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
# ...except the template, which carries no secrets and is the only record of
# what a clone actually needs to set. `.env*` was hiding it from git entirely.
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Local content store for IPFS_MODE=local — real CIDs, machine-local bytes.
#
# Ignoring this is correct NOW and was dangerous BEFORE, and the difference is
# worth writing down because it cost three ENS names.
#
# Under `IPFS_MODE=local` these files were the ONLY copy of the bytes behind every
# `contenthash` record written to Sepolia. A CID is the hash of its bytes, so the
# record is only resolvable while some provider holds them — and the sole provider
# was an untracked directory on one laptop. Three subnames under `atlas-apps.eth`
# (`attested-market-guard`, `wallet-bound-guard`, `aave-health-guard`) lost their
# bytes that way. `ipfs.io` returns 504 for all three and permanently will:
# regenerating the manifest produces a different CID that no longer matches the
# chain, so there is no repair, only a re-publish.
#
# `IPFS_MODE` is now `pinata`, so Pinata is the provider and this directory is a
# cache rather than the system of record — which is what makes ignoring it safe.
# If you ever switch back to `local`, this line is a data-loss bug, not a hygiene
# preference. See the IPFS block in `.env.example`.
.atlas/
.gstack/
